  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "FR-C02-je",
      "sequence": 60,
      "title": "je — \"I,\" the smallest word you own",
      "headword": "je",
      "romanization": "je",
      "gloss": "I",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "sight",
      "derivedModality": "sight",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "sight-cue"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:ff50c0c7a0372bad",
      "notice": {
        "modality": "sight",
        "needs": [
          "your eyes, because the lesson points at something written down"
        ],
        "waitUntilStopped": [],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your eyes, so it is not fully a driving lesson. You will want your eyes, because the lesson points at something written down. You can listen to all of it now and come back to the parts that need looking at once you have stopped."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "To introduce yourself you need \"I.\" French's is tiny — but it comes from a word you already own whole in English."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "je = zhuh — a soft j (as in measure) plus a light uh."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before a vowel it elides: je ai becomes j'ai (\"I have\"). You'll see the same squeeze in je m'appelle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "je — \"I.\" Root: Latin ego. That Latin form survives raw in English:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ego, egoist, egotism, egocentric — the \"I\" at the center of the self."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So the word for \"I\" and the psychologist's ego are the same ancient word — one worn down to a whisper (je), one kept whole (ego). (Spanish wore ego down to yo; French wore it further, to je.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"je\" (zhuh)",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"je\" (*zhuh*)]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the elision — \"je ai\" becomes \"j'ai\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the elision — \"je ai\" → \"j'ai\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the English cousin that kept the Latin whole — ego",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the English cousin that kept the Latin whole — *ego*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What Latin word is je worn down from, and what English word kept it whole? (ego — English ego.) What happens to je before a vowel? (It elides to j'.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "FR-C02-me",
      "sequence": 65,
      "title": "me — \"myself,\" the same word as English me",
      "headword": "me",
      "romanization": "me",
      "gloss": "me / myself (reflexive & object pronoun)",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "sight",
      "derivedModality": "sight",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "wide-table"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:e39268d8a3eb3f22",
      "notice": {
        "modality": "sight",
        "needs": [
          "your eyes for one table that cannot be read aloud"
        ],
        "waitUntilStopped": [
          "Grammar Lens: the reflexive set (me / te / se)"
        ],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your eyes, so it is not fully a driving lesson. You will want your eyes for one table that cannot be read aloud. You can listen to everything else now — leave the section called Grammar Lens: the reflexive set (me / te / se) until you have stopped, and I will say so again when we reach it."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The middle piece of je m'appelle. It's a real word with a real history — not just a bit of glue — and you've owned it in English all your life."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "me = muh — a light uh, like je."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before a vowel it elides: me appelle becomes m'appelle (the e drops, the words fuse)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "me — \"me / myself.\" Root: Latin mē (the accusative \"me\") — literally the same word as English me. The whole family grows from one Proto-Indo-European root me-:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English me, my, mine;"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin mē and meus (\"my\"), which became French mon / ma / mes."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So the tiny m' in je m'appelle is a first-person marker you've carried in English since childhood — me, my, mine are its cousins."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the reflexive set (me / te / se)",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "me is one of three little pronouns that bounce a verb back onto its doer:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "table-skipped",
              "reason": "too-wide",
              "columns": 4,
              "rowCount": 3,
              "headers": [
                "French",
                "means",
                "root",
                "English cousin"
              ],
              "text": "There is a table here I cannot read to you — 4 columns and 3 rows, and it has more columns than can be held in the ear at once. Its columns are: French, means, root, English cousin. Come back and look at it when you have stopped."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You'll use me to give your own name (je m'appelle) and te in the informal \"what's your name?\" (comment tu t'appelles?). Same machine, different person."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"me\" (muh), then its elision — \"m'appelle\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"me\" (*muh*), then its elision — \"m'appelle\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the English cousins from the same root — me, my, mine",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the English cousins from the same root — *me*, *my*, *mine*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the set — me (myself), te (yourself), se (oneself)",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the set — *me* (myself), *te* (yourself), *se* (oneself)]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What Latin word is me from, and what three English words share its root? (mē — me, my, mine.) What are its two siblings, and what does me do to a verb? (te, se; it bounces the action back onto the doer — reflexive.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "FR-C02-appeler",
      "sequence": 70,
      "title": "(s')appeler — \"to call,\" and \"to call oneself\"",
      "headword": "(s')appeler",
      "romanization": "(s')appeler",
      "gloss": "to call / to call oneself",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:8f9ff30215a2498a",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "French says \"my name is\" by saying \"I call myself.\" Here's the verb that does it — and a fat English family riding on it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "appeler = a-play — the -er infinitive ending is a plain \"ay.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In m'appelle the doubled ll keeps a crisp \"l\": ma-PELL."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "appeler — \"to call.\" Root: Latin appellāre (\"to address, call by name\"). The English cousins are everywhere in formal language:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "appeal — to call to a higher authority."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "appellation — a name or title (an appellation of wine is its official name)."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "appellate, appellant — the court that hears appeals, and the one who brings it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "To name someone is, at root, to call them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: reflexive verbs — doing something to yourself",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "French builds \"to be called\" from \"to call\" plus a little word that bounces the action back onto the doer: s'appeler, \"to call oneself.\" That bounce-back word changes for each person:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "me = myself, te = yourself, se = himself/herself."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So \"I call myself\" needs me: je me appelle becomes (elided) je m'appelle. English hides the same move in \"I call myself\" — French just uses it as the normal way to give a name."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"appeler\" (a-play)",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"appeler\" (*a-play*)]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the English cousins — appeal, appellation",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the English cousins — *appeal*, *appellation*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"call oneself\" with me — je me appelle",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"call oneself\" with *me* — *je me appelle*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What Latin verb is appeler from, and two English cousins? (appellāre — appeal, appellation.) What does a reflexive verb do, and which little word means \"myself\"? (Bounces the action back on the doer; me.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "FR-C02-je-mappelle",
      "sequence": 75,
      "title": "je m'appelle… — \"my name is…,\" literally \"I call myself\"",
      "headword": "je m'appelle…",
      "romanization": "je m'appelle…",
      "gloss": "my name is… (literally \"I call myself…\")",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:2086246b35177dc4",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Assemble the two pieces you just met — je and (s')appeler — into the single most useful sentence for meeting anyone."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "unknown",
          "title": "The phrase, assembled",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three atoms, each already taken apart on its own — je (I, from ego), me (myself, from mē), appelle (call, from appellāre):"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "je (I) + me (myself) + appelle (call)."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "me elides before the vowel of appelle becomes m'."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "je m'appelle… = word for word \"I call myself…\" = \"my name is…\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Je m'appelle Arun. becomes \"My name is Arun.\" Je m'appelle Mira. becomes \"My name is Mira.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "French prefers this verb-based \"I call myself\" to a literal \"my name is.\" The literal form does exist:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "mon nom est… (\"my name is…\"), with nom (\"name\") from Latin nōmen becomes English noun, nominate, nominal, nomenclature."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "But mon nom est sounds stiff and official — a passport-desk phrase. For a real introduction, reach for je m'appelle. (You now also see why \"noun\" is literally \"a naming word.\")"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"je m'appelle…\" then your own name",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"je m'appelle…\" then your own name]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "what it literally says (\"I call myself\")",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: what it *literally* says (\"I call myself\")]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the stiff literal alternative — \"mon nom est…\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the stiff literal alternative — \"mon nom est…\"]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does je m'appelle literally mean, and why does me become m'? (\"I call myself\"; elision before the vowel of appelle.) What's the more literal but stiffer \"my name is,\" and what English word is nom cousin to? (mon nom est; noun.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "FR-C02-tu-vous",
      "sequence": 80,
      "title": "tu / vous — \"you,\" familiar and formal",
      "headword": "tu / vous",
      "romanization": "tu / vous",
      "gloss": "you (familiar / formal)",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:3dce6e2a0e409adc",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "To ask someone their name, you need \"you.\" French has two, and choosing between them is a small act of social judgment."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "tu = too, but with the front-rounded French u (lips rounded, tongue forward). vous = voo (silent s)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "tu (familiar \"you\") from Latin tū."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "vous (formal / plural \"you\") from Latin vōs, \"you all.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English once had the same split — old thou was the tu — but thou died out, leaving one flat you for everyone."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: two \"you\"s, chosen by register",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "tu — for friends, family, children, peers. Intimate, equal."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "vous — for strangers, elders, anyone you'd show respect — and it's the plural \"you all.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The trick: French makes politeness by using the plural on a single person. Addressing one respected person as \"you all\" is the polite move (French even has a verb for it, vouvoyer). This is the same politeness Spanish builds with usted, but by a different route: Spanish coined a whole new pronoun (from \"your grace\"); French just borrows its plural. Using tu too early can feel over-familiar — when in doubt, vous."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"tu\" (front-rounded u), \"vous\" (voo)",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"tu\" (front-rounded *u*), \"vous\" (*voo*)]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "who gets tu (friends), who gets vous (strangers, elders)",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: who gets *tu* (friends), who gets *vous* (strangers, elders)]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the rule of thumb — \"when in doubt, vous\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the rule of thumb — \"when in doubt, *vous*\"]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What decides tu vs vous — and what does French do to be polite to one person? (Register — familiar vs respect; it uses the plural (vous) on one person.) What old English word was the lost tu? (thou.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "FR-C02-comment",
      "sequence": 85,
      "title": "comment — \"how,\" from the same quomodo as Spanish cómo",
      "headword": "comment",
      "romanization": "comment",
      "gloss": "how",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:a6b068e356a88846",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "French asks \"what's your name?\" as \"how are you called?\" — so first, the word for \"how.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "comment = ko-MAHN — the final -ent is a nasal vowel; the t is silent."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "comment — \"how.\" Root: Latin quo modo, \"in what manner\" — two words fused and worn into one."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That same quomodo also became Spanish cómo — two Romance languages, one Latin \"in-what-way,\" reshaped differently."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The piece modo (\"manner, measure\") is alive in English mode, model, moderate, modify — all about the way a thing is done."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: French counts names as a matter of \"how\"",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Where English asks \"what is your name?\", French asks \"how are you called?\" — comment (how), not what. The question is about the manner of your calling, not a thing to be named. Don't translate word for word; learn the French logic: the name-question runs on comment."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
