            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Assemble it: \"how\" + \"do you call yourself\" = the polite question that gets the introduction going."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "unknown",
          "title": "The phrase, assembled",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "comment (how) + vous appelez-vous (\"do you call yourself\")."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "comment vous appelez-vous? = literally \"how do you call yourself?\" = the polite \"what's your name?\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: asking by inversion",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A statement — vous vous appelez (\"you call yourself\") — becomes a question by flipping the verb and the pronoun, joined with a hyphen:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "vous appelez-vous?"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That flip is French's formal way to ask anything. The informal version simply swaps in tu:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "comment tu t'appelles? — note te elides to t', exactly as me became m' in je m'appelle."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So the same verb, s'appeler, runs the whole exchange: je m'appelle to give your name, comment vous appelez-vous? to ask for theirs."
            }
          ]
        },
