            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The graceful close to an introduction — and it literally says you've been put under a spell."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "enchanté = ahn-shahn-TAY — two nasal vowels (en, an), then a clean \"tay.\" ch = English sh."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "enchanté — \"delighted.\" Root: Latin in- (\"upon\") + cantāre (\"to sing, chant\"). To enchant was literally to sing a spell upon someone. The magic survives in the English family:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "enchant, incantation, and the plain chant."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So saying enchanté on meeting someone means, gallantly, \"I'm enchanted [to meet you].\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: it agrees with you",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "enchanté follows the gender-agreement rule of bon/bonne from Chapter 1: it reports the speaker's own gender."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A man says enchanté."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A woman says enchantée — an extra -e (same sound, silent)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
